y it's gonna be interesting to see  what is it the total volume of payments  processed in cryptocurrencies of q1 this  year was 40 million dollars right so  it's just it's growing over 400 percent  year over year  it i don't think this will be in place  this year i  maybe next year i read some stuff that  they have an election next year so it's  very up in the air but i see this going  into place  at some point in the future  i also think that there's a really  interesting element to uh in countries  that have the certain political  environments they're leading to some of  the adoption we also see uh this game  theory playing out where which countries  are going to adopt it uh and which  countries will bend the knee which  countries will uh stand up we saw uh  president buchelli and el salvador  basically put the middle finger up to  the imf with remains to be seen whether  a smart move or not uh you could see a  country like brazil  how much are they willing to be defiant  to the global monetary community  and how much of it do they feel like  they are reliant on that global  community for uh gdp growth and and for  making sure that their citizens are okay  lots and lots and lots of questions in  here but to see them  even talking about it remember bitcoin  literally launched january 3rd 2009.  we're talking about in a 12-year period  we now almost 13 years we now have a one  nation state that's adopted at this  legal tender they literally have  onboarded about 50  uh of their citizens we now have another  major country in the world that is  talking about doing this whether they  actually do it or not in some weird way  just them talking about it is already a  win because it begins to normalize the  fact that bitcoin could become legal  tender and over time you're gonna get  younger and younger leaders that step up  into those leadership positions and they  will end up thinking it's just the  default of course bitcoin should be  legal tender and they'll go ahead an